\title{malariafwd: concepts and terms}
\description{
malariafwd simulates \emph{Plasmodium vivax} transmission and genetics forward in time in SLiM, one
host, mosquito and parasite genome at a time, and reads what a run records from R. This page
defines the terms the help pages share.
}
\section{Start here}{

\code{\link[=example_run]{example_run()}} returns a small run that ships with the package, so every function that reads a
run can be tried without SLiM. \code{vignette("malariafwd")} shows how to run a simulation and reads the bundled run,
\code{vignette("parameters")} gives the parameters that are easy to get wrong and how to choose
them, and \code{vignette("anatomy")} walks through what a run records (all three are also on the
\href{https://shaziaruybal.github.io/malariafwd/}{website}); \code{\link[=event_types]{event_types()}} lists every event a run
logs. The model is described in full in
\href{https://shaziaruybal.github.io/malariafwd/MODEL_DESCRIPTION.html}{MODEL_DESCRIPTION}, the
reasons for its choices are in
\href{https://shaziaruybal.github.io/malariafwd/DECISIONS.html}{design decisions}, and the scripts that
validate the model are installed in \code{system.file("validation", package = "malariafwd")}.
}

\section{Runs and their tables}{

A \emph{run} is one simulation: \code{\link[=run_sim]{run_sim()}} writes it to a directory, and \code{\link[=load_run]{load_run()}} reads that
directory back as a \code{vivax_run}. Every function that reads a run takes either. A run's outputs
are four files, each read by its own function: \code{params.tsv}, the parameter set it was made from
(\code{\link[=load_params]{load_params()}}); \code{events.tsv}, one row per event (\code{\link[=load_events]{load_events()}}, described in
\link[=malariafwd-outputs]{events.tsv}); \code{state.tsv}, every host and mosquito on every day
(\code{\link[=load_state]{load_state()}}, hosts by default); and \code{plasmo.trees}, the genealogy of every genome as a tree
sequence (\code{\link[=trees_path]{trees_path()}}). An argument named \code{events} takes an events table or a run, one named
\code{state} a state table or a run, and a plot takes a run or the table its function returns.
}

\section{Time}{

A \emph{tick} is one day. A run's days are 1 to \code{SIM_DAYS}, and every column named \verb{*_tick} is a day.
Most durations are geometric: a state with a mean duration of \code{D} days ends on each day with
probability \code{1/D}. \code{EIP_DAYS} and \code{GAMETOCYTE_DELAY} are fixed delays, \code{RECRUD_DELAY_DIST} sets
the shape of a recrudescence's delay, and with \code{K_LATENCY} above 0 a batch's latency is a
sequence of geometric stages. A year is 365 days, so a rate per person-year divides by
person-days over 365.
}

\section{Genomes and identifiers}{

Each parasite genome is one individual of the simulation. A bite, an import, a relapse and a
recrudescence each make a new genome, and a batch of hypnozoites is one genome that stands for
its count. \code{pedigree_id} names a genome, uniquely within a run; it is 0 or more, and -1 means
none. \code{lineage_id} names a clonal group: a genome keeps its parent's lineage through clonal and
selfed descent, and a recombinant starts a new one. In this package \emph{lineage} always means this
group; the genealogy itself is the tree sequence. \code{oocyst_id} names one meiosis in a mosquito,
\code{human_id} and \code{mosq_id} a host and a mosquito, and \code{entity_id} either of them in \code{state.tsv}.
}

\section{Cases, episodes and acquisitions}{

A \emph{case} is a clone onset, one row of \code{\link[=classify_cases]{classify_cases()}}: a bite or an import that gave a
blood-stage infection, a relapse, or a recrudescence. Founder seeding, where a run starts, is not
a case. An \emph{episode} is the clones that became patent in one host on one day, named by
\code{episode_id}, \code{"<human_id>:<tick>"}, so a bite that delivered two genotypes is two cases of one
episode (\code{\link[=episodes]{episodes()}}, \code{\link[=recurrences]{recurrences()}}). An \emph{acquisition} is a case that began a new blood-stage
clone in its host: an import, a local reinfection or a relapse. A recrudescence is a case but not
an acquisition, since it re-expands a clone already counted. A relapse into a patent clone of its
own lineage is flagged by \code{into_patent_lineage}, and \code{exclude_masked = TRUE} leaves such relapses
out of the counts.
}

\section{COI and MOI}{

Here the complexity of infection (COI) and the multiplicity of infection (MOI) are one quantity:
the distinct patent blood-stage lineages in a host, \code{moi_active} in \code{state.tsv}. \code{MAX_MOI} gates
new inoculations: a host already carrying that many blood-stage lineages, patent or subpatent,
takes no new bite or import. Relapses bypass it, and a bite that delivers several genotypes is
admitted whole, so a host's COI can exceed it.
}

\section{Transmission}{

A \emph{transmission link} joins a parent genome of an oocyst to a genome its products infected:
\code{direct}, through a bite's blood stage, or \code{liver}, through a relapse of the batch a bite left.
\code{\link[=transmission_links]{transmission_links()}} lists them, each with its \emph{chain}, a connected group of links. \emph{Rc}, the
case reproduction number, is the mean number of secondary cases per case, by true origin
(\code{\link[=rc_by_origin]{rc_by_origin()}}). \code{\link[=classify_chain]{classify_chain()}} classes each case by its true chain from an import, and
\code{\link[=classify_travel]{classify_travel()}} by what a rule that knows only travel history would conclude.
}

\section{Relatedness}{

Every genome descends from root genomes, which are unrelated: the founder templates, one per
founder lineage (\code{N_FOUNDER_LINEAGES}), and the imports. The founder hosts of one lineage carry
clonal copies of its template. So the genealogy is a forest. Identity by descent (IBD) is read
from it exactly: \code{\link[=ibd_pairs]{ibd_pairs()}} for pairs, \code{\link[=ibd_segments]{ibd_segments()}} for the stretches behind them, and
\code{\link[=ibd_lookup]{ibd_lookup()}} to read a pair. \code{\link[=arg_tmrca]{arg_tmrca()}} gives how far back two genomes share an ancestor,
and \code{\link[=arg_summary]{arg_summary()}} the shape of the forest.
}

